{
  "biological_interpretation": "Mediator perturbations split by subunit and neuronal context: a Med15/Med13/Med23-sensitive synaptic and dendritic module is not reproduced by the CDK8-module genes Med12 or Cdk8.",
  "caveats": [
    "Med15, Med23, and Med13 mRNA rows were not themselves FDR-depleted in the 151 TH response, so the module is a perturbation-associated signature rather than confirmed transcript dose response.",
    "Genome-wide correlations among Mediator perturbations were weak; the convergence is driven by a restricted marker module."
  ],
  "cell_types": [
    "151 TH Prkcd Grin2c Glut",
    "052 Pvalb Gaba",
    "005 L4-5 IT CTX Glut"
  ],
  "exact_observed_result": "Med15 produced a 151 TH Prkcd Grin2c Glut down-module containing Atp2b1, Lrrc7, Plekhg1, Atp2a2, Grm1, Pcp4, Prkcd, and Kcnc2; Med13 and Med23 overlapped that module on Grm1/Pcp4/Plekhg1/Atp2a2/Prkcd. Med13l contributed only Grm1, while Med12 and Cdk8 were FDR-silent in the same thalamic markers.",
  "preliminary_scores": {
    "novelty": 4,
    "priority": 5,
    "support": 5
  },
  "prior_expectation": "Mediator complex subunits are physically connected through Pol-II transcriptional regulation, but different tail and kinase-module members can have context-specific transcription factor dependencies; the thalamic synaptic down-module refines complex membership into subunit-specific neuronal outputs.",
